#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "bb7ae99a-b8d9-5e4d-906c-9e18d22c1404" :question/math-academy-id "e-8742" :question/problem "Calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf{F}-3\\mathbf{G})$ for the vector fields $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\left(x^2+y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ \\left(xy-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\quad \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{G}=x+y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (k \\mathbf F) = k \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F$\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot (\\mathbf F + \\mathbf G) = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F + \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-3G) & =∇\\cdot (F)+∇\\cdot (-3G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-3∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+x-2y \\\\  & =3x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-3G) & =∇\\cdot F-3∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =(3x-2y)-3\\cdot (x+y) \\\\  & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7de054d4-8384-5aab-b2c4-a43b1f86a16c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8928" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac 1x, xy \\right \\rangle$ and $f(x,y) = \\dfrac 1{x},$ calculate $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ and $\\nabla f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},0⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+x)+⟨\\frac{1}{x},xy⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d2669f-748a-5d3e-add4-044dc27f9818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489595} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2395f7-fab5-51f4-8be4-159b63c3d4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489773} {:answer/id #uuid "c917523d-92c4-54de-b0df-a6bf816b85b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489953} {:answer/id #uuid "d31bc03e-05c6-5840-8321-3067c96b1d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(2-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489983} {:answer/id #uuid "e786bbf5-86e3-5cad-83cf-c31ac0c9a27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c917523d-92c4-54de-b0df-a6bf816b85b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04136be-f727-5add-9a25-13584d652f7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe02f258-a8c1-593e-bacb-46d349ae065b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105634" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (-3F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-4z\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-3F) & =-3∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4z) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-3F) & =-3∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2) \\\\  & =6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dc01da-2660-5cdf-b0fa-a152d2942087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238151} {:answer/id #uuid "2242969b-860c-5835-8b35-57367aa4ec32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238355} {:answer/id #uuid "29cd6443-9f90-57f0-9bcb-d96c14b03760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238423} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa03014-6326-5bd2-b5a3-a79fc531fc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240008} {:answer/id #uuid "f3596578-6003-586e-b0bb-695d533cd500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09dc01da-2660-5cdf-b0fa-a152d2942087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238151} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa9dd5a-693f-5e97-aa2d-7ed5edc6f040" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9703f536-70eb-5a05-8c4a-7f2c7607a6b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105642" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (F+G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F+G) & =∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8e7f9a-6d64-5d22-b5bc-95c71fed9a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz(x+y+2z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489613} {:answer/id #uuid "48f71578-9dc8-5af1-8c23-0a20717bf08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz(x+2y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489684} {:answer/id #uuid "e167ec05-7ba8-54ee-88eb-33b8c7129f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz(x+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490006} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dde9b5-7512-5c76-9578-51e87312e497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz(x+2y+3z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490057} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a0413-baf6-5112-9242-7dcd5ce3749b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz(2x+y+2z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e167ec05-7ba8-54ee-88eb-33b8c7129f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz(x+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490006} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f30e86d-bd3c-561f-bb1d-7773101df310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19772385-86fe-59c3-9b8a-1626c506e897" :question/math-academy-id "q-105656" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨xy,zy,zx⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)=xyz,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(zy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(zx)=y+z+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+⟨xy,zy,zx⟩\\cdot ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}yz \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+xyz(x+y+z) \\\\  & =2xyz(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e772d5fd-8873-5f01-8e0e-9b2d188dbee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(3y+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366852} {:answer/id #uuid "0749c0e0-86d1-5ce9-bc17-1a82f5495ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy(xy+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366853} {:answer/id #uuid "daa2a4aa-b4aa-539e-bf61-98bbcc57db7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y(3y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366854} {:answer/id #uuid "a400b6c4-fad3-550e-9c64-998d075118a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y(y+2x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366855} {:answer/id #uuid "f322dbbd-44f7-50d7-97ec-555e953573ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(3{y}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa2a4aa-b4aa-539e-bf61-98bbcc57db7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y(3y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366854} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5db2c67-62b4-5732-b4f3-224a2f67e30c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf849e13-e8f1-55b6-b51f-70bad727cc07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105743" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨xy,2y⟩$ and $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2xy,{x}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y(y+2)+⟨xy,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨2xy,{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y(3y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "279dea5a-f4d5-586b-a072-0c9f9d03bc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489604} {:answer/id #uuid "760b5f11-f68e-56f1-afde-6e7843ea5c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489788} {:answer/id #uuid "9b53fb88-54d3-5084-89ac-28e6c71dd00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-x{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489861} {:answer/id #uuid "9da03a85-026e-5343-ae06-7ceb351c6a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-6x{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489866} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b21b82-aeaf-5a49-88a9-1e22866dcd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2x{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9da03a85-026e-5343-ae06-7ceb351c6a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-6x{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489866} :answer-field/id #uuid "560133ea-a7b0-52cc-a804-28ad1422872c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d8b3a40-13c2-5419-a866-6118c0d389c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131244" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,i+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\,j-x{z}^{4}\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}F)=\\frac{3}{2}∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y))+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-x{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-4x{z}^{3} \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-4x{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}F) & =\\frac{3}{2}∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-4x{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-6x{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81140528-55a2-55ee-a446-c4ecc4c389dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{y}^{2}+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367368} {:answer/id #uuid "4d99e938-b77d-5e49-9ef0-2b8dd93510e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367369} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf7538b-e8be-53f6-845e-acbc84a6dd44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367370} {:answer/id #uuid "a76b0d37-e266-5f23-bb84-9c2088fc0784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+3y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367371} {:answer/id #uuid "50aebbec-bc0b-570c-b51a-a4d30ffaac7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50aebbec-bc0b-570c-b51a-a4d30ffaac7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367372} :answer-field/id #uuid "f51dbd77-8136-52af-a21a-6a5b89bd8649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e4f4f05-cab9-5b00-b80d-9d3be44e8ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131245" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F+4G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF={x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=-\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F+4G) & =∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F)+∇\\cdot (4G) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}∇\\cdot F+4∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{3}+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}F+2G) & =\\frac{1}{2}∇\\cdot F+4∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x{y}^{3}+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08612da6-00e6-5565-b1b4-b002ee6d449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(xy-1){e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263077} {:answer/id #uuid "185d2e60-1d02-5e5d-a588-c3af72c99d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(y-1){e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263230} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebce6aa-c045-5bdb-b341-49efe839fc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(3y+1){e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263554} {:answer/id #uuid "6d086b17-e4d8-5e48-b924-63b0d3ebeb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(y+1){e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263943} {:answer/id #uuid "fa628dc9-f28d-5e80-8945-778ac8830569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x({y}^{2}+x){e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ebce6aa-c045-5bdb-b341-49efe839fc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(3y+1){e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263554} :answer-field/id #uuid "1afd5b7a-e97d-5670-86ec-c6252b479311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "850b167e-fac7-553f-bd45-68edd2f03d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-131288" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨xy,y⟩$ and $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y},$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y}⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}(y+1)+⟨xy,y⟩\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y}⟩ \\\\  & =xy{e}^{y}+x{e}^{y}+xy{e}^{y}+xy{e}^{y} \\\\  & =3xy{e}^{y}+x{e}^{y} \\\\  & =x(3y+1){e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e083018-a5e8-5516-b3a3-9c427afd469f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489545} {:answer/id #uuid "18fc1a57-09dc-5787-b3e4-8f5ef0b7fc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z({y}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489569} {:answer/id #uuid "48181509-820b-5e87-ba28-4511cc6dfe9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z(x{y}^{2}+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489683} {:answer/id #uuid "53ef7803-126c-5a6e-93c7-7cf843ded9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z({y}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489713} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbc2503-13f3-56f1-9ffe-484d847562c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z(x{y}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18fc1a57-09dc-5787-b3e4-8f5ef0b7fc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z({y}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489569} :answer-field/id #uuid "402aba4f-11b8-5925-805f-969b29ff490f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cb7d692-f924-57ab-bd09-dd8cc202af02" :question/math-academy-id "q-131289" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨{y}^{2}z,{x}^{2}+z,{x}^{2}⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨1,1,-1⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =(x+y-z)\\cdot 0+⟨{y}^{2}z,{x}^{2}+z,{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =0+{y}^{2}z+{x}^{2}+z-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+z \\\\  & =z({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78adaeaa-a91e-53e2-a192-1440d4d41211" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using Linearity Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f355aca-d71f-57d0-ad5b-f6b4c1c3ee42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+y)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368549} {:answer/id #uuid "52387e5c-65fa-59b4-b443-c7ae60751678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368550} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2acff5-dcff-581e-8504-084584b55f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+xy)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368551} {:answer/id #uuid "b5eb34bb-be52-5af3-b44b-0163802a7ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368552} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf6f6e-7586-5d2d-965e-6a5a468c7dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+x)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f355aca-d71f-57d0-ad5b-f6b4c1c3ee42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+y)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368549} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d6b9ea4-bc00-5b64-9f13-831ea284d323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d98d41c-4aeb-5c37-80bb-0d675e8879ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-41842" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (F+2G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{y}^{2}\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F+2G) & =∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot (2G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F+2∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F+2G) & =∇\\cdot F+2∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{y}+2\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{y}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+y)}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44d953c5-21bb-5326-bfac-e793cfe26cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbac9b0-3131-5b68-8747-b8662452e7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489587} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2f2f0d-40d7-5f2d-83f1-2ea1f486329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489614} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9781e6-d514-50c9-8bc5-ef65fd44af25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489650} {:answer/id #uuid "727ac25f-a90a-5dcd-bcbf-04adfe3d45f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489781} {:answer/id #uuid "cc634756-be7d-5a9e-b487-b504317b94d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b9781e6-d514-50c9-8bc5-ef65fd44af25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy(1+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489650} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c37c60e-e97b-5fd7-8819-529b45993a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bc87287-f16b-573d-aff7-5fb6dedac503" :question/math-academy-id "q-98888" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x,{x}^{2}y⟩$ and $f(x,y)=xy,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)=1+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =xy(1+{x}^{2})+⟨x,{x}^{2}y⟩\\cdot ⟨y,x⟩ \\\\  & =xy+{x}^{3}y+xy+{x}^{3}y \\\\  & =2xy+2{x}^{3}y \\\\  & =2xy(1+{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
